Chapter: MetalLadder
  cites Works chapter GopHandoff
  cites Works chapter GopDraw

 A payload's report channel on a board with no serial port and no proven
 ConOut. The screen is the only channel demonstrated to reach a human on the
 ASUS: the stub's own fills render there, which is why this paints rather
 than prints.

 The contract is that the LAST COLOUR STANDING names the last stage passed.
 A stage paints on success and the payload spins on failure, so a dead
 machine still reports. The operator table lives in docs/Hardware/HardwareSitting.md.

 We say:

Section: Palette

 Framebuffer pixels are B,G,R,X in memory order and poke-32 stores
 little-endian, so a 0xRRGGBB literal lands correctly.

  ladder-cyan : Integer = 65535

  ladder-yellow : Integer = 16776960

  ladder-magenta : Integer = 16711935

  ladder-orange : Integer = 16744448

  ladder-white : Integer = 16777215

  ladder-red : Integer = 16711680

 Green is deliberately absent. The UEFI stub holds dark green while it runs,
 so a green screen already means the payload never reached its first rung,
 and a rung painting green would be indistinguishable from that.

  ladder-blue : Integer = 255

Section: Channel

  ladder-paint : Integer -> Integer
  ladder-paint (colour) =
    let b = boot-fb-base 0
    in if b == 0 then 0
    else let f = gop-fill-rect b (boot-fb-stride 0) 0 0 (boot-fb-w 0) (boot-fb-h 0) colour
    in if peek-32 b 0 == colour then 1 else 0

 A HEARTBEAT, for a stage that takes minutes. The rungs report the stage that
 PASSED, so a stage in progress and a stage that died inside it paint the same
 screen: the 2026-08-11 sink flight held ORANGE for both reasons and the
 operator had no way to tell which, which is the whole of what that flight
 failed to establish.

 The bar goes in a BAND below the field rather than over it, so the field still
 carries the last-colour-standing contract and only the band carries motion. It
 clears the band before drawing, so a bar can go backwards when a new phase
 resets it. It answers the painted width, which is what a bed asserts on: a
 heartbeat nobody can measure is the same failure one level up.

  ladder-band-h : Integer = 24

 The width is separated from the painting so it can be tested without a
 framebuffer. Everything that can be got wrong here is arithmetic -- a bar that
 saturates early, one that overflows the panel, one that divides before
 multiplying and reports zero for the whole first half -- and none of it needs
 a screen to catch. `codex/test/apps/ladder-bar-width` is the arm.

  ladder-bar-width : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ladder-bar-width (w) (done) (total) =
    if total <= 0 then 0
    else if done <= 0 then 0
    else if done >= total then w
    else w * done / total

  ladder-tick : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ladder-tick (colour) (done) (total) =
    let b = boot-fb-base 0
    in if b == 0 then 0
    else let w = boot-fb-w 0
    in let h = boot-fb-h 0
    in if h <= ladder-band-h then 0
    else let y = h - ladder-band-h
    in let x = ladder-bar-width w done total
    in let clear = gop-fill-rect b (boot-fb-stride 0) 0 y w ladder-band-h 0
    in let f = if x > 0 then gop-fill-rect b (boot-fb-stride 0) 0 y x ladder-band-h colour else 0
    in x

 ladder-hold repaints rather than spinning on an empty loop. A pure
 non-terminating function is not a halt: the optimizer removed the first
 version of this outright, so a FAILED stage fell through and painted the
 colour that means the stage passed. The framebuffer writes are what make
 this one survive.

  ladder-hold : Integer -> Integer
  ladder-hold (colour) =
    let f = ladder-paint colour
    in ladder-hold colour

 ladder-pass paints and RETURNS on either outcome, so a caller can say what
 it painted before it stops. Holding inside here made a failure mute: the
 hold never returns, so the stage that failed printed nothing at all and a
 held colour was indistinguishable from a run still in progress.

  ladder-pass : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ladder-pass (ok) (colour) (prev) =
    if ok == 0 then ladder-paint prev
    else ladder-paint colour

  ladder-hold-if : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ladder-hold-if (ok) (prev) =
    if ok == 0 then ladder-hold prev else 0
